# Day 22/50 — Data Packets

When we send a large file over a network, it doesn't always travel as one giant piece.
Instead, the data is divided into smaller units called **packets**.

Each packet carries part of the information, along with details that help it reach its destination. 
At the receiving end, the data can be reassembled.

A huge amount of information can travel across networks this way.

And there's something interesting about this approach.

Big things don't always have to move as one big piece.
Sometimes, breaking them into smaller parts makes the entire process easier to manage.

**Because even the biggest journeys can happen one small packet at a time**
